Sorry, but olwm and tvtwm don't do it. They place the title at that position
and the window at a position below it.

This becomes a problem when you want a program to be able to save its current
configuration and restore is later.

Currently, my solution is:

	XCreateWindow(...);
	XSetWMProperties(..);
	XMapWindow(...);
	XFlush(...);
	XMoveWindow(...);


It works with olwm, but is less than elegant. All this leaves me wondering
if I'm overlooking something obvious.,comp.windows.x,sorry but olwm and tvtwm don t do it they place the title at that position and the window at a position below it this becomes a problem when you want a program to be able to save its current configuration and restore is later currently my solution is xcreatewindow xsetwmproperties xmapwindow xflush xmovewindow it works with olwm but is less than elegant all this leaves me wondering if i m overlooking something obvious

